
## The planner

`$package/planner.py` never names an agent. Each `run_goal` call asks the
registry for every skill tagged `content`, lets its model plan the goal over
those skills (at most three steps), and runs the plan. Add another agent with
a `content` skill to `agentframe.yaml` and the planner can use it, with no
code change.

The planner calls the specialists with its own development key, so
`run_goal` requires the `plans:run` permission, which only the development
client holds here, and a plan check refuses longer plans before anything is
sent. A production planner calls the specialists on behalf of the signed-in
user instead: `default_remote=OnBehalfOf(scope=...)` with `EntraIDAuth`.

The planner chooses its model with `model_from_env()`, which needs a model
with structured output. Call it on `http://127.0.0.1:$planner_port` with:

```json
{"goal": "Describe what you want done, in plain words."}
```
